execution evidence belong with CP-3C, and legal consequences with CP-4.","table_display":"1. Cash retention, not just earnings resilience, is the central credit distinction. CP-1A BF01/X04 establishes a direct September borrowing-to-dividend funding link; CP-1 BS14 and SE03 distinguish the accrued obligation from October payment. Debt-funded equity transfers leave fixed claims without an acquired operating earnings stream. This raises leverage-tolerance and extraction risk even when operations improve; it is not a finding of unlawful payment, priming or coercive LME. 2. The franchise offers a credible, conditional business-risk mitigant. CP-1A B03/B04 describes estimated proprietary/aftermarket content and historically stronger aftermarket economics; B10 ties demand to utilization and fleet conditions. Replacement consumption can be more persistent than new aircraft deliveries, supporting margin and cash generation. It is not contracted recurring revenue, verified sole-source share or a cash-flow floor. 3. Historical financial progress did not translate into lower gross carrying leverage. CP-1 K05/K06/K12 shows improving operating performance and cash conversion, while K09 increases between FY2023 and FY2024. A lower mechanical cash-netted multiple is not equivalent to stronger retained liquidity because cash preceded a declared payment and accessibility is unproven. 4. Historical refinancing actions are useful counterevidence, not forward clearance. CP-1A X06/BF02 documents extensions and repricing. These mitigated selected historical funding burdens but did not retire consolidated debt; secured substitution also changes the claims mix. Instrument-level reconciliation and dated execution evidence belong with CP-3C, and legal consequences with CP-4."},{"fact_id":"prose-CP-2-5.r0.c0","label":"Business, ownership and operating economics","table_id":"prose-CP-2-5","row_id":"r0","field":"c0","amount":null,"text":"Structure and control. TD Group is the listed holding com