en total sales rise. The observed annual sales increase must not be labeled wholly organic, price-driven or volume-driven: acquired contributions and a checked price/volume bridge are Not supplied (CP-1A X01/X02/G07).","table_display":"Price, volume and mix. Value-based pricing is management's stated approach, not a quantified FY2024 price contribution. Utilization, fleet age/size, airline economics, OEM production schedules and defense budgets are supported volume mechanisms (B09/B10). Pricing can offset cost pressure only if customers accept increases and regulatory/contract conditions permit them. OEM/aftermarket and defense mix can change consolidated margins even when total sales rise. The observed annual sales increase must not be labeled wholly organic, price-driven or volume-driven: acquired contributions and a checked price/volume bridge are Not supplied (CP-1A X01/X02/G07)."},{"fact_id":"prose-CP-2-5.r3.c0","label":"Business, ownership and operating economics","table_id":"prose-CP-2-5","row_id":"r3","field":"c0","amount":null,"text":"Diversification with correlated exposures. Multiple component families, direct customers, distributors and destination markets broaden demand sources (B02/B05\u2013B07). Note-level customer disclosure supports the absence of an individually dominant customer at the stated reporting threshold; management also reports meaningful top-customer concentration. Such breadth reduces isolated counterparty risk, but commercial aerospace utilization, OEM production and government spending remain common drivers. Distributors can shift stocking ahead of end-user consumption. Destination sales do not reveal functional-currency exposure, cash location or accessibility. This supports diversification as a mitigant, not immunity from sector stress.","status":"known","unit":null,"currency":null,"scale":"1","decimals":null,"period":null,"basis":"Literal wrapped paragraph from the model-authored handoff; no new analysis.","denominator":null,"citat