ure supports the absence of an individually dominant customer at the stated reporting threshold; management also reports meaningful top-customer concentration. Such breadth reduces isolated counterparty risk, but commercial aerospace utilization, OEM production and government spending remain common drivers. Distributors can shift stocking ahead of end-user consumption. Destination sales do not reveal functional-currency exposure, cash location or accessibility. This supports diversification as a mitigant, not immunity from sector stress."},{"fact_id":"prose-CP-2-5.r4.c0","label":"Business, ownership and operating economics","table_id":"prose-CP-2-5","row_id":"r4","field":"c0","amount":null,"text":"Costs and capex. Product teams, decentralized manufacturing and investment in tooling/automation provide potential productivity levers (B11/B12). Conversely, qualified supply chains, specialized labor and certification constraints impede rapid substitutions and can generate expediting, idle-capacity or inventory costs. Historical signed capex is modest relative to operating cash, but the maintenance/growth split and fixed/variable cost structure are Not supplied. Therefore, capex flexibility is bounded: reducing investment cannot be assumed costless to reliability, certification or future competitiveness. Acquisitions are separate cash uses, not capex within CP-1 K02.","status":"known","unit":null,"currency":null,"scale":"1","decimals":null,"period":null,"basis":"Literal wrapped paragraph from the model-authored handoff; no new analysis.","denominator":null,"citations":[{"source_id":"CP-2","source_sha256":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c","locator":"line 70","quote":"**Costs and capex.** Product teams, decentralized manufacturing and investment in tooling/automation provide potential productivity levers (B11/B12). Conversely, qualified supply chains, specialized labor and certification constraints impede rapid substitutions and can generate