supplier of highly engineered aircraft components\u201d; S1 p.1 supplies jurisdiction and headquarters. \u201cLeading\u201d is deliberately not adopted. | Specialized components on commercial and military aircraft create installed-base service demand \u2192 assess recurring replacement demand rather than treat all sales as aircraft deliveries. Platform economics are estimates in B04, not guaranteed orders. |","formula":null,"cached_value":null,"hidden":false,"data_type":null,"number_format":null,"note":null},{"locator":"line 53","text":"| B02 Product mix | Documentary Fact | Power & Control: actuators, ignition/engine technology, pumps/valves, motors, batteries, sensors, hoists and microwave electronics. Airframe: latches, rods/seals, security/displays, restraints, interiors, lighting, parachutes and testing. Non-aviation: ground restraints, space actuators, gas-turbine controls and heavy-equipment refueling. S1 p.5: \u201cProducts are sold in the original equipment and aftermarket market channels.\u201d CP-1 SG04/SG08/SG09 retain segment sales below. | Diverse component families reduce single-product dependency, but segment mix and end-market cycles still affect earnings \u2192 preserve all three segments and do not equate diversification with immunity to aircraft-sector shocks. Individual product revenue/margin shares: Not disclosed. |","formula":null,"cached_value":null,"hidden":false,"data_type":null,"number_format":null,"note":null},{"locator":"line 54","text":"| B03 Revenue-channel estimate | Management Language | S1 p.4: \u201cWe estimate that approximately 55% of our net sales in fiscal year 2024 were generated from the aftermarket\u201d; same page estimates approximately 90% proprietary products. These are issuer estimates, not a new calculation. | Proprietary installed parts and replacement demand can support repeat revenue and pricing discretion \u2192 potentially more durable cash generation, conditional on utilization, certification and competition; not subscription/contracted recurring revenue. Defense includes OEM and aftermarket, so the Note 15 commercial aftermarket rows are not the entire aftermarket estimate. |","formula":null,"cached_value":null,"hidden":false,"data_type":null,"number_format":null,"note":null},{"locator":"line 55","text":"| B04 Aftermarket margin/stability characterization | Management Language | S1 p.4: \u201cHistorically, these aftermarket revenues have produced a higher gross profit and have been more stable than net sales to original equipment manufacturers\u201d; aircraft life estimated approximately 25\u201330 years and product life cycle in excess of 50 years. S1 p.9 explicitly says commercial aftermarket historically produced higher gross profit margins than commercial OEM. | Long platform lives can spread replacement consumption across cycles; channel-mix shifts still move margins \u2192 historical resilience is a business mitigant, not a guaranteed margin or forward cash-flow floor. Aftermarket standalone gross margin: Not disclosed; no invented margin spread. |","formula":null,"cached_value":null,"hidden":false,"data_type":null,"number_format":null,"note":null},{"locator":"line 56","text":"| B05 End markets / concentration | Management Language | S1 p.7 estimates FY2024 commercial/regional/business/general aviation aftermarket approximately 31%, commercial aerospace OEM approximately 27%, defense approximately 40%, non-aerospace approximately 2%; \u201cOur top ten customers ... approximately 42%\u201d; \u201cNone ... greater than 10%.\u201d Note 3 p.121 separately states no customer individually accounted for 10% or more in any supplied annual year. | Customer breadth tempers individual counterparty dependence, but common aerospace/defense drivers can correlate demand \u2192 monitor sector/platform and distributor exposures beyond single-customer thresholds. Approximate narratives are not exact segment-account reconciliations; boundary wording at the threshold is preserved. |","formula":null,"cached_value":null,"hidden":false,"data_type":null,"number_format":null,"note":null},{"locator":"line 57","text":"| B06 Geography | Documentary Fact | S1 p.143: \u201cNet sales are measured based on the geographic destination of sales.\u201d FY2024/FY2023/FY2022 United States sales 5,032/4,265/3,496; foreign 2,908/2,320/1,933 (USD millions). S1 p.121: foreign customers \u201cprimarily in Western Europe, Canada and Asia.\u201d | Destination diversity broadens demand sources but adds FX, export and cross-border operating exposures \u2192 do not infer functional-currency exposure, unrestricted foreign cash or legal asset location from customer geography. Individual-country detail: Not disclosed as material. |","formula":null,"cached_value":null,"hidden":false,"data_type":null,"number_format":null,"note":null},{"locator":"line 58","text":"| B07 Customers / routes to market | Documentary Fact | S1 pp.5\u20137 list OEMs, system suppliers, airlines, maintenance providers, military agencies/depots and distributors. S1 p.6: \u201cWe also use a number of distributors to provide logistical support\u201d; Boeing Distribution Services and Satair are named major distributors. | Direct technical selling and distributor channels connect installed-base demand to orders; distributor stocking can distort near-term shipments \u2192 customer sales are not automatically end-user consumption, and quoted concentration is not platform concentration. Distributor-specific sales: Not disclosed. |","formula":null,"cached_value":null,"hidden":false,"data_type":null,"number_format":null,"note":null},{"locator":"line 59","text":"| B08 Revenue recognition / visibility | Documentary Fact | S1 p.120: \u201cA substantial portion ... revenue is recorded at a point in time basis\u201d; limited customized contracts recognize over time using cost-to-cost inputs. S1 p.121 elects not to disclose remaining performance obligations for contracts with original duration of one year or less. | Point-in-time sales depend on shipments/control transfer; some over-time sales introduce estimation and contract-asset timing \u2192 avoid calling installed-base opportunity backlog or contractual revenue cover. Backlog/order coverage measure: Not disclosed in these passages. |","formula":null,"cached_value":null,"hidden":false,"data_type":null,"number_format":null,"note":null},{"locator":"line 60","text":"| B09 Operating driver \u2014 pricing | Management Language | S1 pp.4, 6 describes \u201cpricing our highly engineered value-added products to fairly reflect the value we provide\u201d; p.7 says certification/development investments \u201cmay create disincentives for potential new competitors.\u201d | Qualification costs may slow switching and permit pricing, while regulatory/customer scrutiny may constrain it \u2192 do not assume unlimited pricing power or classify all sales as sole-source. Price/volume decomposition and sole-source share: Not disclosed. |","formula":null,"cached_value":null,"hidden":false,"data_type":null,"number_format":null,"note":null},{"locator":"line 61","text":"| B10 Operating driver \u2014 volumes | Documentary Fact | S1 p.7: commercial aftermarket demand depends on installed base, RPKs, fleet age/size, warranty mix and airline profitability; defense depends on budgets, campaigns and political pressures. S1 p.8: \u201cshipments and revenues generally run ahead of aircraft delivery schedules\u201d; p.9 describes order/inventory and strike disruptions. | Utilization drives spares; OEM build rates drive initial equipment with timing leads; budget releases drive defense \u2192 distinguish these cash-flow paths in stress work rather than extrapolate one consolidated growth rate. Historical discussion of fiscal 2025 indicators is not adopted as a forecast. |","formula":null,"cached_value":null,"hidden":false,"data_type":null,"number_format":null,"note":null},{"locator":"line 62","text":"| B11 Operating driver \u2014 cost / manufacturing | Documentary Fact | S1 p.6: approximately 120 manufacturing facilities, cross-functional product teams, engineering in cost of sales and selling/admin, R&D in selling/admin. S1 p.9: certification processes \u201ccould prevent efficient replacement of a supplier, raw material or component part.\u201d | Decentralized product accountability supports cost response, but qualified supply chains and specialized labor constrain substitution \u2192 supply interruption and cost inflation can impair margins and working capital even where demand persists. Fixed/variable cost split and unit costs: Not disclosed. |","formula":null,"cached_value":null,"hidden":false,"data_type":null,"number_format":null,"note":null},{"locator":"line 63","text":"| B12 Operating driver \u2014 capex | Analyst Interpretation | CP-1 CF23 signed capex and K02 retained below; S1 p.6 describes equipment, tooling, IT/cybersecurity and automation investment; S1 p.142 reports segment capex. | Cash capex is modest relative to reported operating cash in these periods, but acquisition spend is outside capex/FCF \u2192 historical CFO less capex is not cash available for equity or debt reduction after acquisitions. Maintenance/growth split: Not disclosed; measure Not supplied. |","formula":null,"cached_value":null,"hidden":false,"data_type":null,"number_format":null,"note":null},{"locator":"line 64","text":"| B13 Operating driver \u2014 working capital | Analyst Interpretation | CP-1 CF15\u2013CF21/K03 retains annual cash-use block; S1 p.121 defines contract assets as performance in advance of billing and liabilities as advance receipts; \u201cpayment terms vary by the type and location of the customer.\u201d | Inventory/receivable and unbilled performance absorb cash before collection; advances offset some funding need \u2192 earnings growth does not convert one-for-one into liquidity. Exact turns, payment-day targets, seasonal peak requirement: Not disclosed. No acquisition-affected balance change substituted for cash-flow movements. |","formula":null,"cached_value":null,"hidden":false,"data_type":null,"number_format":null,"note":null},{"locator":"line 65","text":"| B14 Regulatory / labor exposures | Documentary Fact | S1 pp.7\u20138: U.S. Government can suspend/terminate contracts, audit fees and seek repayment; FAA/EASA and OEM certification required. S1 p.10 says approximately 17% of full-/part-time employees union represented. | Qualification creates a barrier but also compliance cost and potential supply interruption; government remedies may reduce revenue/cash receipts \u2192 monitor enforcement and labor events without inventing a dated adverse regulatory or rating action. |","formula":null,"cached_value":null,"hidden":false,"data_type":null,"number_format":null,"note":null},{"locator":"line 67","text":"### Ownership and control","formula":null,"cached_value":null,"hidden":false,"data_type":null,"number_format":null,"note":null},{"locator":"line 69","text":"| ID | Category | Evidence / source boundary | Mechanic \u2192 implication / limitation |","formula":null,"cached_value":null,"hidden":false,"data_type":null,"number_format":null,"note":null},{"locator":"line 70","text":"|---|---|---|---|","formula":null,"cached_value":null,"hidden":false,"data_type":null,"number_format":null,"note":null},{"locator":"line 71","text":"| O01 Holding/operating structure | Documentary Fact | CP-1 T4.2, S1 p.110 identifies wholly-owned TransDigm Inc.; S1 p.131 identifies it as issuer and TD Group/other subsidiaries as guarantors for the 2033 notes. | Consolidated earnings and assets are not synonymous with each obligor's cash or collateral \u2192 preserve structural distinctions for legal and refinancing work. No accounting segment treated as an obligor. |","formula":null,"cached_value":null,"hidden":false,"data_type":null,"number_format":null,"note":null},{"locator":"line 72","text":"| O02 Equity class / holder control | Insufficient Information | Listed common class is established in the profile. S1 p.61 Item 12: beneficial-owner information \u201cwill be set forth\u201d in the proxy. No proxy, shareholder agreement, voting-right schedule or current cap table supplied. | Economic interests, voting control, vetoes and blocking stakes cannot be determined \u2192 do not infer a controlling sponsor, dispersed ownership percentages or alignment from a stock-exchange listing. Measure for controlling/economic stake: Not supplied. |","formula":null,"cached_value":null,"hidden":false,"data_type":null,"number_format":null,"note":null},{"locator":"line 73","text":"| O03 Prior ownership / sponsor | Insufficient Information | S1 p.4 documents inception and acquisition history, not a complete ownership chain. No supported prior-owner percentages or current sponsor brief supplied. | No basis for sponsor willingness, equity support or sponsor-track-record assumptions \u2192 historical issuer actions, not presumed private-equity identity, drive this assessment. |","formula":null,"cached_value":null,"hidden":false,"data_type":null,"number_format":null,"note":null},{"locator":"line 74","text":"| O04 Board process / independence | Insufficient Information | S1 pp.60\u201361 defer audit-committee detail, compensation, director independence and related transactions to the proxy; p.60 documents adoption of ethics codes. | Code existence alone cannot establish board independence, effective challenge or creditor alignment \u2192 request proxy and applicable governance documents; missing evidence is not poor conduct. |","formula":null,"cached_value":null,"hidden":false,"data_type":null,"number_format":null,"note":null},{"locator":"line 75","text":"| O05 Incentive structure | Documentary Fact | S1 p.144: options generally \u201cvest based on ... established operating performance goals\u201d; p.146 provides dividend equivalents on eligible vested options and directors' strike-price reduction treatment. | Equity-linked and distribution-linked benefits can align recipients with equity cash returns rather than debt reduction \u2192 examine incentives without evaluating individuals or presuming improper related-party leakage. Performance goals/weights and ownership percentages: Not supplied. |","formula":null,"cached_value":null,"hidden":false,"data_type":null,"number_format":null,"note":null},{"locator":"line 77","text":"### Transaction and capital allocation summary","formula":null,"cached_value":null,"hidden":false,"data_type":null,"number_format":null,"note":null},{"locator":"line 79","text":"No current proposed transaction was supplied. The historical capital-allocation record combines completed acquisitions, refinancing and special distributions: the annual report explicitly links September 2024 notes and Tranche L proceeds plus cash to the special-dividend declaration (S1, FY2024 Form 10-K, Note 10, PDF p.131), while the associated October cash payment is a separately dated subsequent event (Note 16, PDF p.146). Acquisitions may add operating earnings but require integration and cash-contribution testing; a debt-funded distribution instead leaves fixed claims without an acquired earnings stream. The rows below preserve transaction status, funding links, contrary evidence and missing legal/current feasibility inputs without constructing a current deal or certifying capacity.","formula":null,"cached_value":null,"hidden":false,"data_type":null,"number_format":null,"note":null},{"locator":"line 81","text":"| ID | Category | Type, status, parties / funding / use | Evidence \u2192 risk mechanic \u2192 credit implication |","formula":null,"cached_value":null,"hidden":false,"data_type":null,"number_format":null,"note":null},{"locator":"line 82","text":"|---|---|---|---|","formula":null,"cached_value":null,"hidden":false,"data_type":null,"number_format":null,"note":null},{"locator":"line 83","text":"| X00 Current proposed transaction | Insufficient Information | Type, parties, sponsor, uses, new debt and liquidity effect: Not supplied. No proposed