al aftermarket rows are not the entire aftermarket estimate. |"},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"ac6631243ca71cda10dcdafcc5696a3393c9617b61c49bdfe8e94d2d5a33f955"}]},{"ref_id":"C499","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-1A"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 55"},{"name":"Quote","value":"| B04 Aftermarket margin/stability characterization | Management Language | S1 p.4: \u201cHistorically, these aftermarket revenues have produced a higher gross profit and have been more stable than net sales to original equipment manufacturers\u201d; aircraft life estimated approximately 25\u201330 years and product life cycle in excess of 50 years. S1 p.9 explicitly says commercial aftermarket historically produced higher gross profit margins than commercial OEM. | Long platform lives can spread replacement consumption across cycles; channel-mix shifts still move margins \u2192 historical resilience is a business mitigant, not a guaranteed margin or forward cash-flow floor. Aftermarket standalone gross margin: Not disclosed; no invented margin spread. |"},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"ac6631243ca71cda10dcdafcc5696a3393c9617b61c49bdfe8e94d2d5a33f955"}]},{"ref_id":"C500","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-1A"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 56"},{"name":"Quote","value":"| B05 End markets / concentration | Management Language | S1 p.7 estimates FY2024 commercial/regional/business/general aviation aftermarket approximately 31%, commercial aerospace OEM approximately 27%, defense approximately 40%, non-aerospace approximately 2%; \u201cOur top ten customers ... approximately 42%\u201d; \u201cNone ... greater than 10%.\u201d Note 3 p.121 separately states no customer individually accounted for 10% or more in any supplied annual year. | Customer breadth tempers individual counterparty dependence, but common aerospace/defense drivers can correlate demand \u2192 monitor sector/platform and distributor exposures beyond single-customer thresholds. Approximate narratives are not exact segment-account reconciliations; boundary wording at the threshold is preserved. |"},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"ac6631243ca71cda10dcdafcc5696a3393c9617b61c49bdfe8e94d2d5a33f955"}]},{"ref_id":"C501","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-1A"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 57"},{"name":"Quote","value":"| B06 Geography | Documentary Fact | S1 p.143: \u201cNet sales are measured based on the geographic destination of sales.\u201d FY2024/FY2023/FY2022 United States sales 5,032/4,265/3,496; foreign 2,908/2,320/1,933 (USD millions). S1 p.121: foreign customers \u201cprimarily in Western Europe, Canada and Asia.\u201d | Destination diversity broadens demand sources but adds FX, export and cross-border operating exposures \u2192 do not infer functional-currency exposure, unrestricted foreign cash or legal asset location from customer geography. Individual-country detail: Not disclosed as material. |"},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"ac6631243ca71cda10dcdafcc5696a3393c9617b61c49bdfe8e94d2d5a33f955"}]},{"ref_id":"C502","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-1A"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 58"},{"name":"Quote","value":"| B07 Customers / routes to market | Documentary Fact | S1 pp.5\u20137 list OEMs, system suppliers, airlines, maintenance providers, military agencies/depots and distributors. S1 p.6: \u201cWe also use a number of distributors to provide logistical support\u201d; Boeing Distribution Services and Satair are named major distributors. | Direct technical selling and distributor channels connect installed-base demand to orders; distributor stocking can distort near-term shipments \u2192 customer sales are not automatically end-user consumption, and quoted concentration is not platform concentration. Distributor-specific sales: Not disclosed. |"},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"ac6631243ca71cda10dcdafcc5696a3393c9617b61c49bdfe8e94d2d5a33f955"}]},{"ref_id":"C503","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-1A"},{"n