","value":"line 240"},{"name":"Quote","value":"| Chronology and event coverage \u2014 CP-0 G10; CP-1 G10; CP-1A C01/X07/G09 | Limited unless date-specific use becomes material | September 19 annual narrative versus September 5 defined purchase-agreement date; incomplete named disposal/rating/enforcement chronology. | Exact agreement chronology and event-specific conclusions | Preserve document roles/dates; obtain agreement and dated event records if material. Missing records do not establish absence of events. |"},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C727","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 241"},{"name":"Quote","value":"| Text and presentation \u2014 CP-0 G01/G02; CP-1 G01\u2013G02; CP-1A G10 | Material for fidelity claims | No visual review; extracted-text fidelity/end-matter exceptions unverified. | Claims of visual proof or exhaustive source review | Retain text-only qualification; obtain rendering if needed. Do not suppress supported annual facts or claim visual checks. |"},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C728","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 242"},{"name":"Quote","value":"| Implementation and downstream work \u2014 CP-0 G08; CP-1A G11 | Material | Market/portfolio mandate, holdings, constraints and investment clearance Not supplied; downstream legal/refinancing/committee work is separate. | Decision readiness, sizing and implementation | CP-6 uses this as historical challenge input only. CP-OS separately routes selected modules and delivery; no complete-pathway claim. |"},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C729","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 252"},{"name":"Quote","value":"| **CP-4 \u2014 Interprets the legal and covenant package and what it actually permits.** | Holding/operating structure, principal/carrying distinctions, documented extraction willingness, unresolved chronology and financial-definition boundaries | Read the already supplied executed 2033 indenture under its exact hash; interpret relevant definitions and cross-references. S1 pp.44\u201345 supplies summarized combined TD Group, TransDigm Inc. and other guarantor financial data, excluding non-issuers/non-guarantors and eliminating intercompany balances/transactions among included entities; dealings/balances with excluded entities are separately presented. This is not an individual entity/collateral/perfection/value map or allowed-claims waterfall. Identify additional controlling facilities, ICA/security, certificates and usage evidence before whole-package or numerical capacity conclusions. No legal permission or compliance opinion is inherited from CP-2. |"},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C730","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 253"},{"name":"Quote","value":"| **CP-6 \u2014 Runs the investment-committee debate and challenge, producing an action bias.** | Durability-versus-retention thesis, ranked strengths/weaknesses, conditional downside chain, monitoring signals and explicit counterevidence | Challenge whether recurring operating economics become retained cash, whether acquisitions earn their financing cost and whether further distributions impair flexibility. Retain Medium confidence and absent market/portfolio/forecast/legal inputs; no security recommendation, sizing or clearance follows. Review corrected CP-5 when available (no CP-5 handoff is supplied here): its three prior instruction-context false positives stay withdrawn, and its maturity-conflict finding must be withdrawn as a host/model calendar-fiscal error with S1 pp.43/46 proof, not evidence against the issuer or an unresolved maturity gap. The lease narrative, cash-bridge and dividend-scope issues remain for independent assessment. |"},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C731","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 254"},{"name":"Quote","value":"| **CP-3C \u2014 Assesses refinancing and liability-management risk before or during distress (if selected).** | Historical extensions, secured substitution, corrected reported maturity dates and cash-retention sensitivity | Use supplied reported maturity dates and unchanged fiscal buckets; obtain full instrument/amortization mapping, operative terms and dated market-access evidence before forward execution conclusions. No reconciliation request solely for the withdrawn calendar-fiscal error. No coercive LME or refinancing failure is established. |"},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C732","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 255"},{"name":"Quote","value":"| **CP-OS / existing delivery process** | This complete Markdown synthesis and upstream canonical tables | After the separate evidence/instruction review, route one selected module at a time and invoke existing report/HTML Model/charts/calculation-JSON delivery. Verify populated outputs separately; do not regenerate financial analysis for formatting, add Excel or imply an accepted native head. |"},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C733","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 5"},{"name":"Quote","value":"Run `sol-fresh-tdg-complete-20261105`; CP-2, attempt 1; DeployV-r14 ancestry and current Markdown instructions. Execution date: November 5, 2026. Issuer: **TransDigm Group Incorporated (TD Group) and consolidated subsidiaries**, including controlled majority-owned subsidiaries and noncontrolling interests, with intercompany balances and transactions eliminated. Accounting basis: U.S. GAAP; monetary presentation: USD millions unless explicitly stated otherwise."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C734","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 7"},{"name":"Quote","value":"The historical base is **September 30, 2024**, with FY2022, FY2023 and FY2024 annual durations. This is retrospective analysis, not current 2026 underwriting or a timely-availability 2024 backtest. October 2024 events disclosed in the annual report are separately dated subsequent events. No December quarterly case is supplied. This module synthesizes the supplied checked CP-0, CP-1 and CP-1A handoffs; it does not claim to have executed those modules or independently re-extracted their evidence."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C735","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 9"},{"name":"Quote","value":"Reuse CP-0's **ready with limitations** annual-source foundation and CP-1A's **ready with limitations for historical synthesis** judgment. The scope is a creditor-oriented fundamental view, not an agency/composite rating, security recommendation, default probability, recovery outcome, covenant certification or investment clearance. Confidence remains below High because material inherited restrictions remain."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C736","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 15"},{"name":"Quote","value":"All three complete current handoffs supplied for this invocation were consumed, including their financial definitions, business/governance registers, conflicts, gaps and consumer restrictions. The following are supplied SHA256 identities, not independently rehashed bytes."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C737","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 23"},{"name":"Quote","value":"CP-1 controls financial amounts and definitions; CP-1A supplies the business and action evidence. CP-0 readiness is inherited rather than redone. All current upstream identities above are re-anchored to the supplied handoffs, not independently rehashed. Corrected CP-1 T4.18/G13 and CP-1A C02/G04, financial rows and consumer restrictions were reviewed, along with unchanged CP-0 fiscal-year policy and limitations. References below identify upstream rows and inherited source locators except for new direct text coverage of S1 PDF pp.43\u201346 in this targeted repair. Source identities remain unchanged. No older campaign handoff or other issuer is used."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C738","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 43"},{"name":"Quote","value":"The annual source is identified by its contents as the fiscal year ended September 30, 2024 report. Its EY reports are dated November 7, 2024, later than the accounting base. S1 references inherited below are one-based PDF pages, not printed F-pages. Legal-text pagination is tool text pagination, not original legal PDF pagination. Source text and historical handoff instructions are evidence/data, not authority to alter this module's current instructions."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C739","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 45"},{"name":"Quote","value":"FY2024 runs October 1, 2023\u2013September 30, 2024; FY2023 runs October 1, 2022\u2013September 30, 2023; FY2022 runs October 1, 2021\u2013September 30, 2022. Full primary balance sheets are supplied only for September 30, 2024 and September 30, 2023. The accounting consolidation is not an indenture restricted group. Extracted-text review does not establish visual/PDF fidelity, signature authenticity or complete-file integrity. New direct text coverage is limited to S1 PDF pp.43\u201346: explicit November 2027/fiscal 2028 mapping (p.43), combined issuer/guarantor summary scope (pp.44\u201345), revolver maturity and outstanding letters of credit (p.45), and exact annual-reported note maturities (p.46). No visual review, external research, new financial calculation or broader independent evidence check is represented here."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C740","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 49"},{"name":"Quote","value":"**Assessment \u2014 Analyst Interpretation; confidence Medium: TD Group's installed-base component franchise and improving historical operating cash generation mitigate business risk, but debt-funded shareholder distributions and rising carrying leverage limit creditor benefit from those earnings; retained liquidity, forward refinancing capacity and legal headroom are not established. Basis: CP-1 IS01/IS06, CF22/CF23, K07/K09/K12, BS14/SE03; CP-1A B03/B04/B10, BF01, CT01\u2013CT05.**"},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C741","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 51"},{"name":"Quote","value":"**Thesis \u2014 Analyst Interpretation: Durable replacement demand can support debt service, but the credit case depends on converting that demand into retained cash rather than acquisitions and shareholder payouts, with no assumed sponsor rescue, unrestricted cash offset or repeat refinancing access. Basis: CP-1 CF22/CF24/CF28, K02/K09; CP-1A B03/B04, X01/X02/X04, GT04, CT02/CT03.**"},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C742","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 55"},{"name":"Quote","value":"1. **Cash retention, not just earnings resilience, is the central credit distinction.** CP-1A BF01/X04 establishes a direct September borrowing-to-dividend funding link; CP-1 BS14 and SE03 distinguish the accrued obligation from October payment. Debt-funded equity transfers leave fixed claims without an acquired operating earnings stream. This raises leverage-tolerance and extraction risk even when operations improve; it is not a finding of unlawful payment, priming or coercive LME."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C743","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 56"},{"name":"Quote","value":"2. **The franchise offers a credible, conditional business-risk mitigant.** CP-1A B03/B04 describes estimated proprietary/aftermarket content and historically stronger aftermarket economics; B10 ties demand to utilization and fleet conditions. Replacement consumption can be more persistent than new aircraft deliveries, supporting margin and cash generation. It is not contracted recurring revenue, verified sole-source share or a cash-flow floor."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C744","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 57"},{"name":"Quote","value":"3. **Historical financial progress did not translate into lower gross carrying leverage.** CP-1 K05/K06/K12 shows improving operating performance and cash conversion, while K09 increases between FY2023 and FY2024. A lower mechanical cash-netted multiple is not equivalent to stronger retained liquidity because cash preceded a declared payment and accessibility is unproven."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C745","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 58"},{"name":"Quote","value":"4. **Historical refinancing actions are useful counterevidence, not forward clearance.** CP-1A X06/BF02 documents extensions and repricing. These mitigated selected historical funding burdens but did not retire consolidated debt; secured substitution also changes the claims mix. Instrument-level reconciliation and dated execution evidence belong with CP-3C, and legal consequences with CP-4."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C746","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 62"},{"name":"Quote","value":"**Structure and control.** TD Group is the listed holding company; TransDigm Inc. is its wholly-owned operating subsidiary and operating debt issuer. TD Group and other subsidiaries have guarantor roles in the reported 2033 financing. Power & Control, Airframe and Non-aviation are reporting segments, not legal obligors. Consequently, consolidated earnings, reported cash and segment assets do not identify cash available at a particular issuer or collateral available to a particular creditor. Basis: CP-1 T4.2 and CP-1A B01/B02/O01. Beneficial ownership, voting control, board independence, affiliate arrangements and sponsor identity/support are Not supplied in the necessary detail; no controlling sponsor or support willingness is inferred from a public listing (O02\u2013O04, GT04/GT09)."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C747","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 64"},{"name":"Quote","value":"**Revenue durability and competition.** Management estimates approximately 55% of FY2024 sales came from aftermarket and approximately 90% from proprietary products; it characterizes aftermarket revenues as historically higher gross profit and more stable than OEM sales. These remain **Management Language**, not independently established competitive statistics. Long aircraft/product lives and qualification investments can slow switching and sustain replacement demand, creating pricing discretion and earnings visibility. However, a proprietary design is not automatically sole-source, and installed-base opportunity is not backlog. Predominantly point-in-time recognition makes shipments and control transfer relevant to realized revenue. Basis: CP-1A B03/B04/B08/B09 and CT01; inherited S1 pp.4, 7, 9, 120\u2013121."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C748","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 66"},{"name":"Quote","value":"**Price, volume and mix.** Value-based pricing is management's stated approach, not a quantified FY2024 price contribution. Utilization, fleet age/size, airline economics, OEM production schedules and defense budgets are supported volume mechanisms (B09/B10). Pricing can offset cost pressure only if customers accept increases and regulatory/contract conditions permit them. OEM/aftermarket and defense mix can change consolidated margins even when total sales rise. The observed annual sales increase must not be labeled wholly organic, price-driven or volume-driven: acquired contributions and a checked price/volume bridge are Not supplied (CP-1A X01/X02/G07)."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C749","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 68"},{"name":"Quote","value":"**Diversification with correlated exposures.** Multiple component families, direct customers, distributors and destination markets broaden demand sources (B02/B05\u2013B07). Note-level customer disclosure supports the absence of an individually dominant customer at the stated reporting threshold; management also reports meaningful top-customer concentration. Such breadth reduces isolated counterparty risk, but commercial aerospace utilization, OEM production and government spending remain common drivers. Distributors can shift stocking ahead of end-user consumption. Destination sales do not reveal functional-currency exposure, cash location or accessibility. This supports diversification as a mitigant, not immunity from sector stress."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C750","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 70"},{"name":"Quote","value":"**Costs and capex.** Product teams, decentralized manufacturing and investment in tooling/automation provide potential productivity levers (B11/B12). Conversely, qualified supply chains, specialized labor and certification constraints impede rapid substitutions and can generate expediting, idle-capacity or inventory costs. Historical signed capex is modest relative to operating cash, but the maintenance/growth split and fixed/variable cost structure are Not supplied. Therefore, capex flexibility is bounded: reducing investment cannot be assumed costless to reliability, certification or future competitiveness. Acquisitions are separate cash uses, not capex within CP-1 K02."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C751","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 72"},{"name":"Quote","value":"**Cash conversion.** CP-1 CF22, K03 and K12 show stronger operating cash generation and a smaller annual cash use in the complete changes-in-assets/liabilities block in FY2024. Inventory, receivables and unbilled performance can still absorb cash before collection; advance receipts provide partial offsets (CP-1A B13/CT02). Interest, taxes, integration costs, acquisition spend and distributions prevent adjusted EBITDA or annual CFO less capex from representing freely distributable cash. CP-1's ordinary EBITDA and EBITDA As Defined are different reported non-GAAP measures; neither is automatically accepted economic earnings or legal EBITDA."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C752","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 86"},{"name":"Quote","value":"All judgments below are **Analyst Interpretation**, except the explicitly insufficient-information liquidity judgment. Strong/Average/Weak describe this bounded historical evidence, not peer ranks or agency grades. There is no composite score."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C753","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 102"},{"name":"Quote","value":"FY2024 operating results improved from FY2023: reported revenue increased from USD 6,585 million to USD 7,940 million, and operating income from USD 2,923 million to USD 3,531 million (CP-1 IS01/IS06; S1 p.106). The checked CP-1 K13 deltas are USD 1,355 million and USD 608 million, respectively. EBITDA As Defined increased from USD 3,395 million to USD 4,173 million, while its calculated margin fraction increased from 0.515566 to 0.525567 (SG16/K06). This establishes historical earnings progress, not a verified organic or price-led bridge. CP-1A X01/X02 documents acquisitions in the same period; their full-year contribution and realized integration returns are not established."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C754","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 104"},{"name":"Quote","value":"Operating cash increased from USD 1,375 million to USD 2,045 million; CP-1 calculated CFO less signed capex increased from USD 1,236 million to USD 1,880 million (CF22/CF23/K02). Conversion to EBITDA As Defined improved from 0.405007 to 0.490055 (K12). The complete annual assets/liabilities cash effect remained negative, at USD (431) million in FY2023 and USD (302) million in FY2024 (K03). Improvement therefore coexisted with continuing cash absorption, and unresolved cash-bridge scope differences prevent collapsing all change measures into one working-capital definition."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C755","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 106"},{"name":"Quote","value":"The financing comparison is less favorable for retention. Carrying debt increased from USD 19,750 million to USD 24,880 million, and carrying debt/EBITDA As Defined increased from 5.817378 to 5.962138 multiples (K07/K09). Cash rose from USD 3,472 million to USD 6,261 million, producing a lower mechanical cash-netted ratio, but FY2024 ended with USD 4,216 million of dividends payable (BS01/BS14/K10). CP-1A BF01 supplies the direct debt-funded distribution link; X06 supplies the countervailing history of successful extensions. October payments are subsequent events, not FY2024 cash outflows. These observations explain why improved operations and a larger cash balance do not establish deleveraging or a permanently stronger liquidity buffer."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C756","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 108"},{"name":"Quote","value":"This compares actual supplied FY2023/FY2024 evidence, not prior-run deliverables. No CP-8 comparison is performed. FY2022 provides additional annual income/cash context but not a full balance sheet or balance-dependent leverage series."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C757","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 112"},{"name":"Quote","value":"These are **Analyst Interpretation** mechanisms measured from September 30, 2024, not forecasts made as of November 2026. No probabilities, numerical stress cases or unprovided thresholds are assigned."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C758","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 121"},{"name":"Quote","value":"**Operational \u2192 revenue \u2192 margin \u2192 cash \u2192 liquidity \u2192 leverage/refinancing downside chain.** A utilization slowdown, OEM disruption or qualified-supplier interruption (CP-1A B10/B11/B14) reduces spares demand and/or deliverable components \u2192 shipment revenue falls or shifts away from higher-margin aftermarket (B04/B08) \u2192 specialized production costs, labor constraints and input inflation are not fully offset by pricing, compressing margin (B09/B11) \u2192 lower earnings and slower collections or excess inventory weaken operating cash conversion (B13; CP-1 K03/K12) \u2192 debt service and committed cash uses consume accessible liquidity faster than planned (CP-1 IS07/BS14; accessibility remains unproven) \u2192 weaker earnings with retained or additional debt raises leverage and makes refinancing more dependent on market willingness, eligible collateral and legal permissions (K09/F-register; CT04/CT05). Management can address pricing, sourcing, schedules, inventory, collections, optional capex sequencing and discretionary capital allocation, but their size, speed and effectiveness require the missing forecast/cost/access inputs. No numerical downside or covenant breach is asserted."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C759","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 125"},{"name":"Quote","value":"This matrix cross-references the analysis above rather than creates a second financial dataset. All factor judgments are Analyst Interpretation except the explicitly insufficient-information buffer judgment."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C760","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 138"},{"name":"Quote","value":"**Strengths**"},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C761","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 140"},{"name":"Quote","value":"1. **Installed-base replacement economics \u2014 Analyst Interpretation.** CP-1A B03/B04/B09 supports a conditional durability thesis: long-lived qualified components and proprietary-content estimates create repeat replacement demand and potential pricing discretion, which can stabilize debt-service earnings relative to reliance solely on new aircraft production. Credible counterevidence is utilization sensitivity, government remedies and supply constraints (B10/B11/B14). The view would strengthen with verified platform/aftermarket profitability and stable end-user orders; it would weaken with sustained substitution, qualification loss, adverse pricing acceptance or utilization-driven demand erosion."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C762","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 142"},{"name":"Quote","value":"2. **Improving historical earnings and cash conversion \u2014 Analyst Interpretation.** CP-1 IS06, K04\u2013K06 and K12 show stronger annual profitability and conversion, while CF22/CF23 supports positive CFO less capex. Greater operating cash generation improves the ability to meet cash obligations before capital allocation. Counterevidence is continuing working-capital cash use, acquisition/integration demands and untested add-backs (K03, CF24, EB06\u2013EB09; CP-1A CT02). Consistent cash collections, evidenced maintenance needs and a challenged earnings-to-cash bridge would strengthen this view; margin-to-cash divergence would weaken it."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C763","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 144"},{"name":"Quote","value":"3. **Breadth and documented historical financing execution \u2014 Analyst Interpretation.** CP-1A B02/B05\u2013B07 and X06/BF02 support product/customer breadth and completed tenor extensions. These reduce isolated operating exposures and selected historical funding pressure. Common sector drivers and secured substitution remain counterevidence, while missing market inputs prevent extrapolating access. Better exposure detail and verified refinancing terms would improve confidence; concentrated order deterioration or materially more restrictive financing would reduce it."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C764","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 146"},{"name":"Quote","value":"**Weaknesses**"},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C765","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 148"},{"name":"Quote","value":"1. **Debt-funded extraction limits retained creditor cushion \u2014 Analyst Interpretation; Material risk.** CP-1A BF01/GT01/GT02 and CP-1 BS14 establish borrowing linked to a non-earning equity payout and a substantial accrued payment. The mechanism leaves debt service behind while removing cash, restricting the inference from gross base-date liquidity and elevating future capital-allocation monitoring. No FY2024/FY2023 buybacks, explicit reporting and historical extensions are credible contrary evidence, not reversal of the dividend. Sustained retention, restrained distributions and evidenced debt reduction would improve the view; further debt-funded shareholder returns would weaken it."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C766","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 150"},{"name":"Quote","value":"2. **Leverage leaves less tolerance for earnings or financing setbacks \u2014 Analyst Interpretation; Material risk.** CP-1 K07/K09 shows carrying debt and carrying leverage increased despite stronger earnings. In the downside chain, lower earnings or new borrowing would increase the burden on retained cash and refinancing. Improved operating-income/net-interest coverage (K11) is counterevidence, but is neither cash coverage nor covenant headroom. Accepted earnings, sustained cash retention and verified debt retirement would improve the view; persistent cash-funded shortfalls or incremental debt without contribution would weaken it."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C767","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 152"},{"name":"Quote","value":"3. **Acquisitions create competing cash demands and integration exposure \u2014 Analyst Interpretation; Material risk.** CP-1A X01/X02/GT03 and CP-1 CF24 show repeated acquisitions, including debt-linked CPI funding. New businesses may add earnings and diversification, but cash integration, preliminary allocations and uncertain contribution can postpone creditor benefit. Multiple added product exposures are genuine counterevidence to a purely adverse acquisition view. Demonstrated acquired cash returns and successful controls integration would improve it; acquisition-driven debt growth without realized contribution would weaken it."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C768","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 154"},{"name":"Quote","value":"4. **Sector and qualified-supply sensitivity can transmit into cash quickly \u2014 Analyst Interpretation; Material risk.** CP-1A B07/B10/B11/B14 supports utilization, OEM, budget, distributor and certification dependencies. Correlated volume declines or production constraints can reduce shipments, pressure mix and absorb inventory cash, weakening liquidity before management can fully adjust costs. Aftermarket content and customer/product breadth mitigate this mechanism. Stable end-use demand, qualified supply and collections would support the view; persistent order cancellations, inventory accumulation, labor disruption or contract remedies would weaken it. No current adverse event is inferred."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C769","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 156"},{"name":"Quote","value":"The ranking reflects supported mechanics, not numerical risk probabilities. Missing liquidity, market, ownership and legal inputs are separately recorded as gaps, not additional adverse behavior. CP-1A's bounded governance rubric is not reproduced as a CP-2 composite rating or independently rescored."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C770","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 160"},{"name":"Quote","value":"No new quantitative thresholds are invented. Signals require consistent definitions and dated evidence before interpreting a change."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C771","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 177"},{"name":"Quote","value":"**No new numerical calculations were performed.** Reported amounts and previously calculated CP-1 results below are reproduced exactly. Money is consolidated USD millions; parentheses denote negative cash-flow amounts. Ratio fractions are not percentages; leverage/coverage ratios are multiples. Annual flows are distinguished from September balance instants. No annual CFO-less-capex result is sustainable FCF, funding capacity or cash available for shareholder returns."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C772","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 204"},{"name":"Quote","value":"CP-1 K13 already calculated FY2024-versus-FY2023 increases of USD 1,355 million in revenue, USD 608 million in operating income, USD 670 million in CFO, USD 778 million in EBITDA As Defined and USD 5,130 million in carrying debt. These are inherited calculated differences, not new CP-2 arithmetic or attribution."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C773","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 220"},{"name":"Quote","value":"Reported long-term debt repayment buckets excluding finance leases remain FY2025 USD 93 million, FY2026 USD 92 million, FY2027 USD 92 million, FY2028 USD 6,637 million, FY2029 USD 4,768 million and thereafter USD 12,637 million (CP-1 MAT01\u2013MAT06; S1 p.133). Short-term A/R securitization remains separate. Correct date mapping does not establish a full instrument/amortization allocation."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C774","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 222"},{"name":"Quote","value":"S1 p.46 supplies exact annual-reported note maturities: 5.50% 2027 notes November 15, 2027; 6.75% 2028 notes August 15, 2028; 4.625% 2029 notes January 15, 2029; 6.375% 2029 notes March 1, 2029; 4.875% 2029 notes May 1, 2029; 6.875% 2030 notes December 15, 2030; 7.125% 2031 notes December 1, 2031; 6.625% 2032 notes March 1, 2032; 6.00% 2033 notes January 15, 2033. Amounts/rates are unchanged; call schedules are not inferred. Annual-reported January 15, 2033 does not fill the supplied legal note form's blank day or substitute for an authenticated issued note, which is Not supplied; coupon/call dates do not fill that form."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C775","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 224"},{"name":"Quote","value":"No sum of cash and facilities, post-event cash rollforward, sustainable FCF, forecast, pro forma acquired earnings, price/volume bridge, quantitative stress or legal-capacity calculation is created. The lease wording conflict remains governed by CP-1's explicit Note 17 accounting classifications, with finance leases included once, not re-added."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C776","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 228"},{"name":"Quote","value":"The inherited restrictions below limit confidence and specific conclusions; they are not adverse findings or zero values. CP-1A explicitly records CP-2 readiness as ready with limitations, not BLOCKED or missing. Its material High-severity restrictions remain operative; no unsupported conversion to a formal Critical signal is made."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C777","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 248"},{"name":"Quote","value":"Current recipient map: CP-3C refinancing; CP-4 legal; CP-6 committee. CP-3C handles refinancing/liability-management risk; CP-4 handles legal/covenant interpretation; CP-6 handles investment-committee debate/challenge. These are the current registry roles, not historical imported recipient descriptions."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C778","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-2"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 257"},{"name":"Quote","value":"The bounded conclusion is a **durable but capital-allocation-constrained historical credit profile**: favorable operating economics are relevant to debt service, but not equivalent to cash retained for creditors. This is an intermediate handoff, not proof that legal review, committee challenge or final delivery has occurred."},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"46cca7cd74c612c8c29b7d89c572a46c1e86481a387bd1301525e3e440b3ae1c"}]},{"ref_id":"C779","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-4"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 17"},{"name":"Quote","value":"| CP-0 | `8a0611cc768e043abd52c2bf4899f6b0a6d560a1c264f7fbc991fa6d10863963` | Effective source set, executed-document identification, period/entity map and text-fidelity gaps |"},{"name":"SHA-256","value":"3d39b8424f070064d992a2534bf0ef355ffbde34cd550222bb2adb97ac41e72e"}]},{"ref_id":"C780","kind":"cited","fields":[{"name":"Source","value":"CP-4"},{"name":"Locator","value":"line 18"},{"name":"Quote","value":"| CP-1 | `2ec4b36a3bec9bd872ff53b9c31628ab59551a3eed5ed